{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he shall give to the priest the sacred portion</b> — a thing fit to be sacred, for he does not pay him money but rather unconsecrated produce, and it becomes terumah; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 22:14). Now the reasoning of the verse is: when he eats a sacred portion, he adds its fifth to that sacred portion, and he gives the priest this sacred portion, which is the principal and the fifth. And because Scripture calls it \"sacred\" (קֹדֶשׁ), we learn that it becomes like terumah in a thing fit for that. And this is the correct interpretation, both in the plain sense and in the midrash. Thus Ibn Ezra wrote (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 22:14): \"And he shall give to the priest the sacred portion\" — its meaning is \"with the sacred portion,\" or the word \"and he shall give\" (וְנָתַן) draws itself and another along with it, as he also says: and he adds its fifth upon it, and he gives to the priest, and he gives the sacred portion. But in my opinion there is no need for the word \"upon it\" (עָלָיו)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:45.189353167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}